# A bounded graph core for computer algebra · `spec:nrz:cas-design`

This design fixes the first executable nrz-cas slice of (`spec:nrz:cas-concept`). One heterogeneous graph stores mathematical syntax, original occurrences, contexts, requests and checked identity applications. Budgeted arbitrary-precision rational arithmetic, deterministic bounded search, portable witnesses, scalar differentiation, Jacobians and rational square-free decomposition execute in the first slice. Integration, sums, series, limits, solving and spectra have typed representations and explicit unresolved outcomes. No numerical evaluation, physical interpretation or consumer migration is included.

The concept remains unchanged. In particular, positive logical path costs, exploratory expansion, calculus as the first route, retained conditions, complete original traversal and independent witness replay remain requirements. This document chooses engineering policies rather than adding mathematical completeness claims. Its new labels belong to NRZ in area nrz; the future package belongs to CAS in area cas.

## Package and admission · `sec:nrz:cas-design-package`

**Decision (Independent package)** · `dec:nrz:cas-design-crate`

Create package `nrz-cas` at `crates/cas`, inheriting workspace version, edition, license, publishing policy and every workspace lint. Add the member and a workspace dependency on petgraph in its 0.8 release line; the implementation gate resolves the admitted version and its permitted transitive closure. The two new direct dependencies are petgraph and num-bigint in its 0.5 release line with default features disabled. Num-bigint supplies vetted arbitrary-precision unsigned integers; its expected runtime transitive closure is num-integer and num-traits, with any build-time support resolved in the gate lock. No existing engine crate is a dependency. The rational invariants and arithmetic contract remain local; num-rational is not used. Standard-library ordered containers and a small explicit binary codec require no serialization package, hash package or proof engine. Source verification against the installed dependency and the gate's exact lock bytes occurs in the CAS-foundation step (`step:nrz:cas-foundation`); this document does not claim a locally fetched petgraph API audit.

The public facade reexports documented value types and checked methods from private modules. The proposed file division is `lib.rs` for the facade, `number.rs` for exact coefficients, `model.rs` for tags and handles, `graph.rs` for graph admission and occurrence walks, `context.rs` for declarations and predicates, `rule.rs` for the closed primitive registry, `proof.rs` for proof construction and replay, `wire.rs` for canonical encoding, `search.rs` for bounded exploration, `calculus.rs` for derivatives and Jacobians, `polynomial.rs` for finite coefficient jets and square-free decomposition, and `budget.rs` for counters and errors. A module may be split internally to satisfy lint and review limits without changing this boundary. No public petgraph index, mutable node weight, unchecked expression constructor or arbitrary rule callback escapes.

Implementation adds CAS to `.linter/owners.toml` with a partition whose pattern is `[ %s"templates/" ] %s"crates/cas" [ "/" *VCHAR ]`. It adds all six existing owner activations to `.linter/policies.toml`: `labels.mints-well-formed`, `labels.mints-kind-conform`, `labels.mints-unique`, `labels.heads-conform`, `labels.citations-local-resolve` and `owners.reach-conform`. The matching six CAS entries in `.linter/lists.toml` each have an empty allowances array. No existing activation, allowance, owner or citation reach changes. The CAS README and source use locally resolving CAS labels; these NRZ design labels are cited by repository documents, not through a new cross-owner exception. Test labels follow their actual unit or integration location. Code is AGPL-3.0-only and documentation/data CC0-1.0.

The literal README template and render, its document-manifest row, and the generated census and census-dependent README are updated when the crate lands. The generator's behavior and existing packages remain unchanged. Cargo.lock remains the seed file until the verification letter supplies exact replacement bytes. This design round adds only this template, its render and its manifest row.

## Graph, syntax and source evidence · `sec:nrz:cas-design-graph`

**Definition (Storage and identifiers)** · `def:nrz:cas-design-storage`

Use `petgraph::graph::DiGraph<Vertex, Edge, u32>` as append-only storage. Nodes and edges are never removed or mutated after publication; a new context or changed expression is a new node. The compact directed multigraph permits ordered operands and multiple typed links between the same vertices. StableGraph's removal support is unnecessary. Expression edges form a DAG, proof-dependency edges form a DAG, and checked relation edges may form cycles, as required by (`def:nrz:cas-graph-model`). Traversal never interprets arbitrary graph adjacency as a mathematical proof.

Every published object has a private checked u64 logical serial, assigned in increasing deterministic allocation order within its kind. Token-owned high-water marks survive every rollback, so discarded objects leave permanent gaps and their captured handles never authenticate later objects. Retained occupancy is counted separately and restores with the graph. Private maps translate these serials to petgraph indices; the serial never contains or derives from an index. Batch order is source/declaration order, then child-before-parent structural order, then the ordered rule/match schedule. Overflow is refused before admission. Public handles carry an unforgeable store token plus the serial and kind; a private shared ownership token permits foreign-store rejection without a global counter. Runtime token identity is not mathematical identity and is omitted from Debug, ordering keys, witnesses and exports. Deserialization builds fresh handles after validating portable local references. Even structurally identical stores reject each other's live handles.

**Table (Vertex and edge vocabulary)** · `tab:nrz:cas-design-graph-kinds`

|Vertex|Payload and permitted links|
|------|---------------------------|
|Source|Immutable UTF-8 bytes, caller source key and admission ordinal; no mathematical conclusion.|
|Occurrence|Source/span or generated provenance, expression link and ordered child-occurrence links; distinct even when syntax shares.|
|Expression|Operator tag, sort, scope signature, scalar literal or symbol metadata; numbered Operand links.|
|Binder|Bound sorts, binder convention and optional display names; Body and numbered Bound links; display names do not determine meaning.|
|Context|Field, domain/branch policy, declarations and canonical assumption set; Declaration and Assumption links.|
|Condition|Typed predicate with ordered arguments and optional bound scope; PredicateOperand links.|
|Request|Operation, context, roots, target/ranking and effective limits; Input, InContext and Goal links.|
|Application|Rule/version, relation, substitution and direction; ConclusionLeft, ConclusionRight, Premise and InContext links.|
|Proof|Primitive application, composition or derived expansion; ordered DependsOn links and a proved judgment.|

Edges are an enum with the roles just named, a checked position where applicable and no arbitrary user-defined meaning. Every role has an endpoint-kind table checked at admission. Operand positions are consecutive from zero and unique for one expression; premise order follows the rule schema. Source provenance and original child links are never replaced by equality links. Relation metadata lives on Application vertices, so a multi-premise identity is one checked inference even though its storage has several edges. Read APIs return ordered records by role/position/logical serial and never rely on petgraph neighbor iteration order.

**Definition (Structural keys and sorts)** · `def:nrz:cas-design-interning`

Interning uses the observable ordered index of (`dec:nrz:cas-backend-representations`), keyed by exact structural records: operator discriminant and complete payload, mathematical sort, canonical semantic context header, scope signature, scalar/constant/declaration key and ordered child logical IDs. The complete payload includes exponents, branches, extents and selection conventions, so x² and x³ cannot collide. Identifiers are case-sensitive UTF-8 byte strings without normalization, limited by the byte budget; distinct declarations may have the same display name but have distinct caller keys. Scalar operations remain binary and ordered at construction. Associativity, commutativity, constant folding and cancellation require checked identities rather than changing the original syntax key. Equality of keys is structural sharing alone. No equality classes are merged.

Sorts are Integer, Scalar over a named field, Boolean, Function with an ordered signature, Matrix with scalar field and checked mathematical extents, and Set over a sort. Integer-to-scalar embedding is explicit. Extents are positive or zero integer literals or declared integer extent parameters with their predicates; positivity required by a particular operation becomes its condition. The first executable coefficient field is Q, embedded into real scalar expressions for real differentiation; complex and named field contexts are representable but their unimplemented algorithms return Unsupported. Matrix multiplication, scalar multiplication and entrywise operations have different tags. Rational and real scalar contexts do not equate matrix products by scalar commutativity. No physical dimensions, measures or axes occur here.

The expression vocabulary includes rational literals; free and bound variables; Pi and E as exact named real constants; Neg/Add/Sub/Mul/Div; integer and general powers with separate conventions; named functions with signatures; derivative and integral expressions; finite sums; formal or analytic series; limit expressions; predicates and solution sets; matrix literals/comprehensions, transpose, ordered multiplication and spectral selections; and algebraic-root requests. Operator nodes retain necessary domain and branch data. Unknown valid requests stay typed objects. An algebraic-root request stores a rational polynomial, its variable, field, multiplicity request and selection predicate or requested ordered-root index; it does not assert existence or a unique selected number before that condition has a proof. Pi is never stored as a rational approximation.

**Rule (Original occurrences and complete walks)** · `rule:nrz:cas-design-occurrences`

Admission accepts a flat, child-before-parent batch of expression and occurrence drafts. Each source occurrence has a source ordinal and an optional half-open byte span whose ends are UTF-8 boundaries within the retained source. A missing span is explicit; a generated occurrence has no source span and instead names its derivation. Reusing a source key with different bytes is invalid. Each original occurrence has one parent except roots; repeated syntax needs repeated occurrence vertices, even when their expression links coincide. The draft graph therefore cannot disguise a shared source occurrence as two visits. A source may be supplied by checked builders or the original-expression parser below.

A source may also enter through `source::parse_original(cas, source_key, text, declaration_header, budget)`, returning an `OriginalForm` through the same atomic source/expression/occurrence draft transaction. This source factory is now the declared original-expression parser; it creates no second expression type and supplies no traversal receipt or mathematical equality. Its text grammar, also stated in the CAS README, is:

```ebnf
expression     = sum ;
sum            = product, { ("+" | "-"), product } ;
product        = unary, { ("*" | "/"), unary } ;
unary          = ("+" | "-"), unary | power ;
power          = atom, [ "^", exponent ] ;
atom           = number | name, [ "(", [ sum, { ",", sum } ], ")" ]
               | "(", sum, ")" ;
exponent       = signed_integer | "(", signed_integer, [ "/", digits ], ")" ;
number         = digits, [ ".", digits ], [ ("e" | "E"), signed_integer ] ;
signed_integer = [ "+" | "-" ], digits ;
digits         = digit, { digit } ;
name           = (letter | "_"), { letter | digit | "_" } ;
digit          = "0" ... "9" ;
letter         = "A" ... "Z" | "a" ... "z" ;
```

ASCII whitespace may separate tokens; a number and each signed integer are contiguous. The complete input must match. Binary operators associate left; powers bind above unary signs and have exactly one literal exponent. A fraction denominator must be nonzero. Ordinary `1/2` retains `Div` and both original operands; decimals and scientific tokens become exact `Operator::Rational` values. A directly prefixed negative numeric token becomes a signed rational unless a power intervenes: `-2^2` retains `Neg(IntegerPower(2))`. Parentheses and unary plus widen the enclosed occurrence's span without adding an operator. Leading/trailing whitespace is outside the root span.

Names resolve by exact declaration key in the supplied admitted header, including inherited declarations; display names do not create aliases. Numeric literals use that header's scalar field, with no implicit sort coercion. Calls retain ordered arguments and their declared signature. Reduced exponents fitting `i64` use `IntegerPower`; all other exact rational exponents use `GeneralPower` with the header's explicit `Branch` and an original rational exponent operand. An integer exponent is operator payload, so it has no separate operand occurrence; a general exponent's occurrence span includes its optional parentheses.

The parser's recursive-descent productions suspend on explicit heap continuation frames. The existing `Budget` limits grammar/operand depth, total original occurrences, numeric literal bytes, source/name bytes, candidate storage, arithmetic and work before consumption; raising depth does not introduce native-stack recursion. `ParseError` borrows the source key and offending name, records the offending byte span and first unconsumed byte, and distinguishes stable syntax codes (`UnexpectedToken`, `UnclosedParenthesis`, `TrailingOperator`, `InvalidLiteral`, `InvalidExponent`), unknown names, non-callable names, arity and unchanged underlying `Fault` values. Failure leaves the store unchanged while retaining consumed work. Consumer migration and simplifier changes are outside this parser capability.

`walk_original(root, limits)` produces a deterministic postorder sequence with root-relative operand paths, binder-entry records and source locations, visiting every occurrence exactly once and every child position. It uses a bounded explicit stack. Canceled operands, zero factors and inactive syntactic alternatives are all visited. A quantified body is visited once as syntax, with its binder environment; there is no implicit sum expansion. Only a complete walk mints a root/store/generation-bound TraversalReceipt. Search and calculus requests on original roots require that receipt. Exhaustion gives a partial diagnostic sequence but no receipt. A receipt proves coverage, never physical validity (`rule:nrz:cas-conditional-witnesses`). Generated roots have a checked provenance receipt from their complete construction; exporting them as a new original input for a client requires a fresh occurrence materialization and walk.

Walk work is charged as frames and events are produced, with each reached immutable interpretation record charged once per walk. Its record charge is at least twice that record's logical payload, so a walk reaching the entire store retains the former conservative full-store floor. Unrelated records contribute no work. Staged payload checks cover the walk's own live frames, retained events, dependency frontier, visited-record set and encoding scratch.

**Construction (Capture-safe scope operations)** · `constr:nrz:cas-design-substitution`

Bound references use a de Bruijn depth and slot into an explicit stack of binder sort frames. Free references use declaration keys, never display names. A scope signature is the ordered stack of bound sorts; it participates in interning, so open terms from incompatible frames cannot share. Binder bodies see the new frame; a sum's bounds and a limit's approach point see the outer frame. Matrix comprehensions bind row and column slots in a specified order. These conventions fix off-by-one and shadowing behavior without a fresh-name oracle. Alpha-renaming changes occurrence display metadata only; it preserves the mathematical key.

Free substitution is simultaneous: traverse the old term, replace only the declared free keys, lift free-standing bound indices in each replacement by the current binder depth, and do not recursively substitute inside inserted replacements. Replacements must have the destination free declarations, sorts and initial scope signature. Bound instantiation replaces the selected depth/slot, shifts replacement references when passing nested binders and decrements references to frames removed by that instantiation. Every shift and decrement is checked and every visit charged. The CAS-foundation step (`step:nrz:cas-foundation`) executes free substitution and the generic checked scope machinery; it does not perform beta reduction, sum unrolling or integral substitution. A free variable called x inserted beneath a binder displayed as x remains free. The tests use different and equal display names, nested frames and deliberately dangling indices. This specializes the substitution requirement in (`credit:nrz:cas-rewriting-literature`) without importing an implementation.

**Definition (Canonical mathematical context)** · `def:nrz:cas-design-context`

A context key contains field/characteristic, real or complex domain convention, all power/logarithm branches, free declarations sorted by caller key with their sorts and function signatures, and a sorted duplicate-free set of assumption predicates with their declared hypothesis keys. Predicate terms use structural keys and de Bruijn scopes; conjunction is flattened and sorted, while disjunction and quantifiers retain structure. Different hypothesis provenance is not erased merely because predicates coincide. Predicate kinds include Defined, NonZero, Positive, NonNegative, IntegerRange, Differentiable, Equal, Symmetric and a typed named predicate for unimplemented facts. Contexts are immutable. The operation's bound frames are explicit alongside the context, not ambient mutable state.

The semantic header consists of the field, domain/branch conventions and declarations, computed before assumption terms. Expression keys include this header; the full context key then adds the assumption predicates built under it. This avoids a cyclic key definition. Two contexts differing only in hypotheses may share literal syntax, but applications, proof judgments, derivative caches and search states always name the full context. Such sharing never transports an equality or discharges a guard across those contexts. A changed function signature or branch convention changes the header and therefore the expression keys.

Canonicalization is syntactic, not a decision procedure for logical equivalence. `x>0` and a distinct assumption `x!=0` do not silently identify two contexts. A bounded condition checker proves rational signs, literal equalities, nonzero products/integer powers from nonzero factors, positivity implying nonzero, and recursive definedness for the executable scalar grammar. Exact assumptions discharge identical predicates only as hypotheses of Gamma; their provenance remains in the result. Direct contradictions, such as rational zero declared nonzero or both P and its explicit negation, produce InvalidContext. Unrecognized consistency stays Unknown, recorded in every result; discharged relative to Gamma never means that Gamma has a nonempty model. A residual predicate, an assumption and a derived proof are different evidence variants.

**Rule (Transactional admission and bounded storage)** · `rule:nrz:cas-design-transactions`

All public construction is staged. Validate a flat draft batch, its handles, sort/scope relations, acyclicity by backward references, spans, keys and predicted node/edge counts before assigning published IDs. Public draft admission checkpoints node and edge counts, retained payload, source and original-occurrence counters, and the bounded per-kind retained-count map. An undo journal records new logical indexes, structural interning keys, source keys and occurrence-parent claims. On any error, including mid-batch Work exhaustion, remove appended edges and nodes in reverse order, undo those insertions and restore every checkpoint. No partially checked node remains on failure. Spent Work and allocated serials are not refunded; a handle captured by an internal transaction callback stays invalid after its object is removed. An inner successful publication remains reversible by its enclosing transaction. Earlier handles remain valid and keep their identities. Admission work charges the pending encoded payload, a fixed counter-checkpoint allowance and the logical key probes and validation actually performed. It does not copy or charge unrelated retained records. Existing store occupancy plus pending payload and journal reservation still obey the unchanged storage ceilings. Private proof producers that actually fork a store continue charging their copy.

All input-controlled lengths, sums, products, indices and capacity requests are checked before allocation. Library-owned vectors use fallible reservation; capacity, u32 index and u64 serial limits are preflighted before calling petgraph insertion. Petgraph storage allocation and standard-library allocation failure can terminate the process under Rust's allocator contract; that environmental failure is not a recoverable mathematical outcome. The no-panic contract forbids input-triggered panic paths, recursive stack exhaustion, unchecked indexing, overflow and panic-based error conversion. It does not promise recovery from host termination or an allocator abort. Capacity-overflow inputs and all service-budget refusals are errors as values. This distinction avoids claiming that petgraph supplies a fallible allocator API it does not promise.

## Exact coefficients · `sec:nrz:cas-design-numbers`

**Decision (Budgeted arbitrary-precision rationals)** · `dec:nrz:cas-design-rationals`

Use a private sign plus coprime arbitrary-precision unsigned numerator magnitude and positive denominator. Retained magnitudes use the CAS-owned exact layout in (`dec:nrz:cas-retained-number-layout`); dependency big integers are arithmetic scratch. Zero has positive sign and denominator one. Integers are the denominator-one subset; all public constructors reduce and check the invariant. The sign/magnitude choice makes negation independent of any machine signed-minimum case. Every number reports its measured component bits. The component-bit ceiling defaults to 4096 and the intermediate-bit ceiling to 8192; checked request overrides can raise or lower both without a fixed representation ceiling. Allocation lengths, limb counts and work estimates still require checked machine-size conversions. Values outside the active budget are resource refusals, not floating replacements or claims of mathematical undefinedness (`rule:nrz:cas-exact-domain-policy`).

Addition first divides denominators by their gcd, performs checked unsigned products and signed-magnitude accumulation, then reduces. Multiplication/division cross-cancel numerator/denominator pairs before checked products. Division by zero is InvalidArithmetic; intermediate or final magnitude overflow is Exhausted with ArithmeticRange and the operation location. Every bigint operation preflights a conservative result-bit bound against intermediate_bits and reserves work proportional to a conservative quadratic limb-operation bound before calling the underlying operation. Actual measured result bits are checked as well; a reduced admitted component must satisfy component_bits. Clones, digit conversion and comparisons are charged by measured size. A conservative preflight may refuse a route whose eventual cancellation would fit, which is an explicit resource refusal. A mathematically representable result can still be refused if this bounded route needs an oversized intermediate. Euclidean gcd consumes a step for each remainder. Ordering uses Euclidean continued-fraction comparison instead of overflowing cross multiplication; each division is charged. Powers use checked repeated squaring with a bounded integer exponent, and preserve domain conditions when acting on expressions.

The literal reader accepts an optional sign, decimal digits, an optional decimal point and signed decimal exponent, or a signed integer numerator followed by a slash and unsigned nonzero integer denominator. It rejects mixed forms, malformed syntax, zero denominators and non-ASCII numeric characters. It counts bytes before scanning, checks decimal exponent magnitude before forming powers of ten, strips harmless decimal zeros where possible and performs exact reduction. No float is parsed. Serialization uses the sign and canonical length-prefixed little-endian magnitudes, so decimal spelling does not alter number identity.

**Decision (Exact retained number layout)** · `dec:nrz:cas-retained-number-layout`

Choose an owned sign and two canonical magnitudes: zero and one have inline tags, and every other magnitude owns a minimal little-endian `Box<[u32]>` with a nonzero highest limb. Use u32 limbs because the dependency exposes portable u32 digit import/export, avoiding a retained layout tied to its target-dependent internal limb width. The denominator is nonzero, the components are coprime, and zero has positive sign and denominator one. Inline constants need no allocation and provide allocation-free constant construction. All retained Rational values, including certificate endpoints and nested coefficients, use this representation; a graph-only wrapper would leave the same gap in proof children. Arithmetic uses temporary BigUint/BigInt values and exports the reduced result before retention. No dependency integer or spare-capacity digit vector remains hidden behind the retained type. This replaces the current `Ratio<BigInt>` backing in `crates/cas/src/number.rs`, not the mathematical, component-bit or canonical-wire contracts.

The exact-layout option gives the owner a native layout and length independent of dependency internals. The alternative of retaining dependency integers with an allowance is rejected: the public [BigUint API](https://docs.rs/num-bigint/0.5.1/num_bigint/struct.BigUint.html) supplies bits and digit conversion, but no capacity or supported bound on spare backing; a multiplier of encoded length, a shrink request or the current private limb implementation is no guarantee across upgrades. Keeping CAS-owned Vec limbs is another viable observed-capacity design under (`rule:nrz:cas-backend-exact-allocation`), but retains spare capacity; exact boxed storage gives immutable numbers a simpler retained projection. A custom arithmetic implementation would remove scratch conversion at substantial algorithmic cost without improving the retained projection. Boxed limbs therefore separate the capacity obligation from arithmetic implementation at a small, explicitly charged conversion boundary.

Keep the native Rational exactly 72 bytes on the supported x86_64 target by adding explicit inline padding to the sign and tagged magnitudes. Use `struct Rational { core: Core, _pad: [u8; 72 - size_of::<Core>()] }`, or an equivalent construction whose padding width follows the core layout. Enforce `size_of::<Rational>() == 72` with a compile-time assertion beside Rational and preserve its existing alignment of 8; a core larger than 72 is a compile-time failure. Padding is real retained storage: count all 72 bytes in the enclosing native record, including every reserved vector slot, rather than excluding padding from the physical inventory. This is the smaller change because native size participates both directly and through enclosing types in existing byte envelopes. No separate logical-slot constant or six-site tariff replacement is needed; existing `size_of::<Rational>()` expressions and ``test:unit:lc-omega-correspondence-resource-boundaries`` remain unchanged.

This preserves the direct envelopes in bindings LC correspondence accounting/translation pool, readings sources/map and normalization/storage, and matrix binding/live/expanded and live/coordinates/lowering. It is expected to preserve indirect inline-size figures; the existing pins verify that expectation: ExactConstant in `crates/bindings/src/readings/normalization/storage.rs`, whose exact profiles are pinned by ``test:integration:coordinate-normalization-resource-boundaries`` in `crates/bindings/tests/conformance/coordinate_normalization.rs`; LcTimeTail in `crates/bindings/src/lc_relation/time.rs`, through PiZeroTail's four Rationals; and polynomial Term in `crates/cas/src/solving/coefficients/polynomial.rs`. Keep the existing Draft size pin at 328 and all other enclosing-layout and logical byte pins. The implementation verifies these existing controls alongside the compile-time number-size assertion; a compiler layout change is not permission to revise an envelope. Enclosing enums and options, including Value, Scalar, Operator/Draft, ScalarKernelLaw and Option<Rational>, can depend on niches that the 72-byte assertion does not fix. Padding the number is therefore an expectation of enclosing-size preservation, not a proof of it; the unchanged pins must pass without replacement tariffs.

For component bit length b, let e(b) = ceil(b/8) and l(b) = 4 ceil(b/32). Then l(b) <= e(b)+3, including b=0; inline zero/one only reduce actual backing. Thus for numerator/denominator bits n,d and native inline size h = 72, `h+l(n)+l(d) <= 78+e(n)+e(d) <= 128+e(n)+e(d)`. The disjoint component remainder `128+e(n)+e(d)-72 = 56+e(n)+e(d)` also covers the limbs without borrowing the container's inline slack. For the other envelope, `ceil(b/32) <= 2 ceil(b/64)` gives `l(b) <= 8 ceil(b/64) <= 16 ceil(b/64)`, so the actual 72-byte native slot plus the existing 16-byte-per-64-bit-limb allowance covers the new value. The pool's 130-byte constant envelope is the n,d <= 8 specialization. These are retained-value coverage arguments, not authority to hide simultaneous conversion scratch; that scratch follows the allocation timeline and existing operation scope.

**Rule (Number backing before publication)** · `rule:nrz:cas-retained-number-capacity`

The number owner exposes a nonallocating borrowed projection of each retained component: inline/owned kind, native limb count, checked backing bytes and allocation identity supplied by the retaining owner. An owned component of k limbs has requested layout and observed retained backing `k*size_of(u32)`; inline zero/one has zero backing. The rational header belongs to its enclosing native record once. The [Box layout contract](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/boxed/index.html#memory-layout) makes the slice layout authoritative; no unobservable spare vector slots survive as box capacity. This is a native allocation projection, not allocator metadata or RSS, and does not use wire length as a proxy (`rule:nrz:cas-backend-capacity`).

Every constructor, parser, decoder, arithmetic result, independent clone and replacement that can become retained must first produce a checked allocation plan, then allocate, then observe the resulting owned slices before publication. For a reduced scratch result, k is `bits/32 + (bits%32 != 0)`, except inline zero/one, determined before allocating retained limbs; a larger operation-level reservation may use its conservative result-bit bound, then refine after reduction. A clone plans from the borrowed source layout. All products, sums, bit bounds and host-size conversions are checked before allocation. Moving an existing number requests zero new backing and preserves its identity; an independent clone gets new identities even when equal. Replacement keeps old and new allocations simultaneously visible until actual release. Scratch used to discover the result does not become retroactively covered by the retained plan.

The argument is structural: every retained magnitude is either inline or a slice whose complete native layout is owned and observable; exhaustive dispatch accounts for both components, and no dependency-owned integer can escape into retention. Private constructors establish reduction and canonical limbs before the projection is trusted. Observation verifies the plan after allocation and before publication; failure drops unpublished allocations and preserves prior values and spent Work. The projection feeds requested growth and observed inventory separately under (`rule:nrz:cas-backend-rollback`). It does not authorize an earlier unbounded conversion peak. In particular, a Vec-to-box route must inventory the temporary vector and any simultaneous shrink/replacement, not assume that boxing was in place. Use the exact reservation, initialized fill and boxing timeline of (`rule:nrz:cas-backend-exact-allocation`); the Vec reservation is fallible and the documented boxing conversion retains the environmental allocation-failure policy of (`rule:nrz:cas-design-transactions`). Unsafe initialization is not authorized. Dependency arithmetic scratch remains separate under (`open:nrz:cas-backend-allocation-envelopes`).

**Rule (Retained number conversion Work)** · `rule:nrz:cas-retained-number-work`

Prepay the added passes at their owning number boundary on the caller's Work ledger, before copying or conversion. For k u32 digits, charge k digit visits plus `4*k` copied bytes for each import to arithmetic scratch and each export to retained limbs: `5*k` per pass. A direct independent slice clone not already covered by an existing copy charge prepays `4*k` copied bytes; an additional unpaid validation scan charges its k visits. A pass replacing an already-charged copy is not an added pass: `Rational::negated` in `number.rs` clones both magnitudes under C(B), whose existing charge covers those copied bytes. Keep that floor without charging the replacement slice copy again, including when `sub` invokes negation. Only import and export conversions absent from the old route add `5*k` each; identify old coverage before computing the delta. Any intermediate digit buffer, initialization or second copy adds its own visited digits/copied bytes by the same units. Inline tags have no limb-copy cost; their existing operation floors remain. This literal linear charge covers the explicit adapter passes only: existing conservative bigint operation charges, gcd steps, comparisons, wire encoding and semantic visits remain floors. Neither a capacity observation nor moving an allocation charges a copy. Checked size accounting is not a new per-store scan tariff. No pass may be hidden in an infallible Clone on a budgeted retaining path; such paths require a prepaid copy or a budget-bearing adapter.

These are number conversion costs, separate from retained-map maintenance in (`rule:nrz:cas-backend-work`), and do not settle (`open:nrz:cas-backend-adjacent-work`). A producer that stays entirely in arithmetic scratch exports only when its result crosses the number boundary; it must not repeatedly convert internal bigint intermediates. Existing charges do not decrease when a previous dependency clone disappears. Implementation derives each changed whole-call Work pin from the actual new passes and preserves spent prefixes and exact/one-below refusal. The affected scope is every Work pin whose path constructs, parses, compares, copies or computes a Rational, in cas, bindings and matrix. Examples include number construction/parsing/arithmetic and canonical bytes; graph literal admission, copying and rollback; proof construction/replay and rational polynomial/solving/series operations; exact enclosure and Machin certificate construction/reuse; and bindings/matrix LC construct, replay, authentication and origin Work boundaries that call those routes. Bindings readings, sensitivity, constants and decision paths also belong to that scope, as does `coefficient.mul` in `crates/matrix/src/binding/live/expanded.rs`. These examples are not an exhaustive caller list or invented replacement totals; the implementing change supplies the individual old/new derivations. No byte ceiling, default, dimension, logical byte pin, carrier allocation or unrelated tariff changes; the padded native layout and unchanged physical assertion follow (`dec:nrz:cas-retained-number-layout`). Existing helper reservations and the original STORE figure remain as required by (`rule:nrz:cas-backend-work`); if representation layout or conversion scratch forces a pinned byte change, stop with that path's evidence rather than widen it.

