`Limits::default()` defines these exact values. `LimitsOverride` has an optional value for every row; resolution happens before source admission or a request starts and the complete effective limits are returned. A store has fixed storage/depth/name ceilings chosen at creation; a request may lower them or choose larger operation/work limits, but cannot raise that store's ceilings. To raise a store ceiling, create a fresh store and import under new checked limits. All counts fit u64; platform conversion and petgraph's u32 sentinel exclusion impose additional checked hard caps. Work and count limits may be zero for boundary tests, except quotas and match_batch must be positive; component_bits must be positive and intermediate_bits must be at least component_bits; all estimates and host-size conversions are checked. A zero storage limit permits an empty store and refuses the first object. Request limits do not reset on a retry inside that request. Separate public calls start fresh work counters but retain store occupancy.

The ledger checks before consuming the next unit. Exactly the limit is permitted, limit plus one refuses, and arithmetic used to account for work itself never wraps. A byte comparison charges its inspected bytes; tree-map access also charges comparisons and one container operation. Any comparison implementation that cannot accept the ledger reserves a conservative checked worst-case charge before entering that bounded comparison and records that charge, so interning and queue ordering have no unmetered repeated loop. Sorting reserves a checked quadratic comparison bound on its already bounded input, rather than assuming a particular library sort implementation. Polynomial loops charge coefficient pairs and long-division iterations individually. Memory rows bound logical payload, not allocator metadata or resident-set bytes; the simultaneous old state, candidate state and working queues are all accounted for in their respective rows. This is finite service work, not a wall-clock deadline or a promise to use all requested capacity.

Receiving-store physical backend coverage remains a separate owning-layer design obligation under (`req:nrz:lc-graph-cas-backend-prerequisite`), scheduled by (`step:nrz:backlog-cas-backend-inventory`). Logical payload limits above do not bound later collection capacities or allocator metadata; the prerequisite must specify their inventories or justified allowances and private checked-record registration controls before the consumer can claim complete admission/residence.

Replay uses the same effective size, arithmetic, depth and proof ceilings with a separate checker ledger. BudgetUsage reports generation and replay counters separately; arithmetic_steps and condition_steps cap each ledger, whose aggregate work cap is work for generation and replay_work for checking. All checker invocations within one request share that single replay ledger, so checking successive candidates does not reset its allowance. Heuristic exploration cannot consume the checker's reserved work. Each candidate recorded as best has already replayed successfully; if later generation or replay exhausts, only a previously checked candidate may be returned. Immutable VerifiedWitness records do not need a redundant second replay merely to export them. A standalone replay call starts a fresh checker ledger.

A result envelope has a fixed small bounded error header available even when output_bytes is zero; no witness is emitted when it will not fit. The output limit covers optional payload, while the minimal status, reason, effective limits and fixed-size counters remain available. A best proof whose export does not fit is retained only by an in-store checked handle and explicitly marked NotExported; portable proof delivery requires a larger output budget. Formatting, measuring and canonical encoding still consume the relevant ledger and cannot loop after it is exhausted.

## Receiving-store backend inventory · `sec:nrz:cas-backend-inventory`

**Decision (Observable retained representations)** · `dec:nrz:cas-backend-representations`

Replace the receiving store's BTreeMaps outright with sorted unique vectors, preserving complete keys, equality and canonical order under (`rule:nrz:stable-collection-order`). Keep the existing DiGraph and its public node/edge capacity interface. Serial counters increase independently by kind and survive rollback (`def:nrz:cas-design-storage`); therefore logical indexes store only live `(LogicalId, NodeIndex)` pairs in kind/serial order. Neither a greatest-serial array nor a vector of all discarded serials is admitted. Binary search costs logarithmic comparisons, insertion/removal costs linear worst-case movement, and growth may relocate all live entries. The maintenance rule below charges only an operation's local entry or replacement cost while global-state-dependent charges await (`open:nrz:cas-global-index-search-work`). This is a subsequent implementation contract, not a claim that the current BTreeMaps already expose capacities.

The retained-map increments change only receiving-store retained-map maintenance Work, upward under the rules below. The separately specified number representation adds only its conversion/copy Work under (`rule:nrz:cas-retained-number-work`). No byte limit, ceiling, default, dimension, unrelated charge or matrix-loans origin tariff changes. Affected whole-call Work pins must be revised openly from operation-count derivations and exact/one-below evidence. The earlier opaque map-operation charge does not authorize a new global-index comparison, shift or relocation tariff.

**Rule (Capacity, children and identity)** · `rule:nrz:cas-backend-capacity`

For a vector allocation a of native element type T, the observable backing projection is `capacity(a)*size_of(T)`, with checked multiplication and checked aggregation. Count initialized and unused slots. The vector header belongs only to its enclosing record; enumerate every initialized element's out-of-line children separately. Strings use actual byte capacity; boxes/slices use their retained native layout, with shared control storage under its named supported allowance. Rational limbs use the owning number-layer projection in (`rule:nrz:cas-retained-number-capacity`). An encoded length is not a backing capacity. The projection describes requested/observable storage, not allocator metadata or process RSS (`rule:nrz:plan-resource-ownership`).

Each row has a CAS-authenticated allocation identity comprising owner scope, collection role and allocation generation, plus capacity, layout, retaining edges, lifetime and coverage kind. Moving a buffer preserves identity; replacing it creates a distinct allocation. Shared child allocations count once; independently allocated equal children count separately. References contribute their own inline edge fields, never another pointee. Inventory observation must itself be nonallocating or have inventoried scratch. Public inventory exposes no mutable graph access or address-based mathematical identity. Inline Cas and initial ownership allocations remain distinguished from later backings; `STORE = size_of(Cas)+384` is not a growth allowance.

