Each initialized graph payload variant needs exhaustive child dispatch. Shared environments, canonical keys and exact certificates must remain visible as retaining edges even when their bytes are attributed elsewhere. Existing draft, admission-target, occurrence-uniqueness, wire/key and proof scratch stay in their separate phase inventories. Any opaque child without a supported capacity or justified existing allowance remains an uncovered row and blocks a complete claim; this table does not turn a logical payload tariff into a physical measurement.

**Rule (Exact allocation of retained backings)** · `rule:nrz:cas-backend-exact-allocation`

Every retained Vec/String backing is created or grown with `try_reserve_exact` on an empty or live container, with checked lengths and byte products before the call. The owner chooses the requested amount from its growth policy, for example `target(n+1)` under (`inv:nrz:cas-backend-target-coverage`) or a batch's predicted node and edge counts; exact reservation does not mean allocating one extra slot on every push and causing quadratic relocation. Set additional a to the chosen requested total minus live length n when growth is needed. For native element size s, old observed capacity c, live length n and additional a, preflight coexistence `c*s + (n+a)*s` before growth; an already adequate backing needs no new allocation. Fill only within reserved capacity. [Vec exact reservation](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/vec/struct.Vec.html#method.try_reserve_exact) and [String exact reservation](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/string/struct.String.html#method.try_reserve_exact) avoid deliberate excess; allocator grants are classified separately below. Forbidden allocating routes for retained backings are amortised `reserve`/`try_reserve`, push growth without prior exact reservation, `with_capacity`, `vec!`, allocating `collect`/`clone`, and `shrink_to_fit`. Copy into an explicitly reserved destination instead. Copying inline elements or cloning a shared retaining edge within that destination does not authorize hidden child allocation; each owned child follows its own rule.

For collections outside (`inv:nrz:cas-backend-target-coverage`), shrink only through [into_boxed_slice](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/vec/struct.Vec.html#method.into_boxed_slice) and back with `Vec::from`; a String uses [String::into_boxed_str](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/string/struct.String.html#method.into_boxed_str) and [Box<str>::into_string](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/primitive.str.html#method.into_string), preserving valid UTF-8 without a re-validation scan. Preflight `c*s + n*s` before a shrink that may replace the allocation. Boxing discards excess capacity and the resulting slice has exactly its native length layout; conversion back to Vec preserves that exact capacity. For number limbs, reserve exactly k u32 slots fallibly, fill them, and observe c before boxing. If c=k, boxing needs no reallocation; otherwise preflight `4*c + 4*k` before boxing. Fixed-layout Box children reserve their pointee layout before construction and expose children separately. The documented boxing path needs no unsafe initialization. A supported exact retained result does not remove the old backing from the peak before release.

This rule supplies requested growth and shrink envelopes for the V/S/B portions of every row in (`tab:nrz:cas-backend-capacity-audit`), with the graph exception below and shared Arc controls still open. Child creation, replacement and nested checkpoints are aggregated on one timeline; checkpoint copies include the live source beside their new requested destination. Rollback shrink/replacement peaks must be prepared before the fallible attempt, so restoration does not require a new unpaid reservation. The second increment implements checked plans and observations; F alone enforces their disjoint aggregate on its original ExecutionAccount scope under (`rule:nrz:cas-backend-work`). The rule adds no new byte dimension or unrelated Work tariff.

Current routes needing migration include `State::record` in `crates/cas/src/graph.rs` (`try_reserve(1)`) and graph growth through `add_node`/`add_edge` there, which must be preceded by the exact graph reservation below. Journal.retained is a logical checkpoint copy: at the current source, `State::checkpoint` already calls `RetainedMap::checkpoint`, which uses `try_reserve_exact(target)` followed by inline entry clones in `graph/retained_map.rs`; preserve that explicit route and add its aggregate preflight, rather than introduce a backing `clone`. The generic allocating Clone route is not authorized for a retained checkpoint. Pending's `restore_entries` in `crates/cas/src/decision_kernel/cache.rs` and Accounting::forget in `crates/cas/src/decision_kernel/cache/accounting.rs` already use boxed-slice round trips; add their old-observed-plus-new-length peak plans before rollback needs them. Accounting::restore's push must have prepaid exact capacity too. These sites are migration evidence, not a claim that all current callers obey the new discipline.

**Decision (Observed excess and allocator internals)** · `dec:nrz:cas-backend-allocator-excess`

Preallocation checks the exact requested figure and its coexistence with old observed storage. After the allocation returns, record the full capacity reported by `capacity()` and admit that observed figure before publication, including any excess above the request. Report requested and observed quantities under the same allocation identity, but never subtract reported excess from retained storage. If the observed amount cannot be admitted, refuse and release the unpublished candidate, or shrink exactly with the old-observed-plus-new-length peak preflighted before shrinking; observe and admit the resulting backing before publication. This also governs graph growth and rollback: excess left in a live backing must remain covered, or a previously prepared reversible replacement must restore covered storage. Refusal alone does not make a surviving allocation disappear.

In the second increment, “admit” here means recording the full observed figure in the inventory with checked arithmetic; its only refusal at this boundary is an unrepresentable product or aggregate. Refusal against a byte dimension and its remedy are F's evidence, not a new debit on the second increment's paths. Exercise excess through the pure planning and observation function using synthetic requested/observed pairs: std Vec with the global allocator today does not report allocator excess through capacity(), so allocator luck is not a test oracle.

For collections governed by (`inv:nrz:cas-backend-target-coverage`), an exact shrink to live length n by boxing is not a remedy: n=5 requires target(n)=8. F instead grows into a separately reserved destination, preserving the old covered backing until the new observed backing is admitted; refusal drops the destination and keeps the source. Alternatively, reducing spare capacity uses a fresh exact reservation of target(n) plus a copy, with old-observed-plus-new-request coexistence preflighted, and only replaces the old backing after the new observation is admitted. In-place growth consumes the old backing and cannot provide this refusal fallback. If the replacement's observed amount also fails admission, retain the old covered backing and refuse. These choices settle F's target-preserving remedy design; the reported-excess branch is not reachable with std's global-allocator Vec today and is exercised with synthetic planning/observation pairs. They do not add a new byte check in the second increment.

Exclude only the allocator's internal block choice that `capacity()` does not report, not usable capacity reported by the collection. The transient excess inside the allocation call itself stays under the environmental-allocator boundary of (`rule:nrz:cas-design-transactions`), with allocator overhead explicitly excluded from this measurement under (`rule:nrz:plan-resource-ownership`); it is not a promise to pre-bound the allocator's internal peak or process RSS. An observation cannot retroactively authorize that transient, but it is mandatory evidence for admission of the returned retained backing. The argument is that the caller controls a request while the collection reports the storage actually available afterward: preflight the former, enforce retention against the latter, and keep only unreported allocator internals outside (`rule:nrz:cas-backend-capacity`). This preserves a conservative retained inventory under (`req:nrz:lc-graph-cas-backend-prerequisite`). Retention, transfer and rollback preserve the observed reservation until actual release.

**Decision (Exact graph reservation with environmental failure)** · `dec:nrz:cas-backend-graph-allocation`

Keep DiGraph and use [petgraph 0.8.3 exact node and edge reservation](https://docs.rs/petgraph/0.8.3/petgraph/graph/struct.Graph.html#method.reserve_exact_nodes) before insertion. Preflight old observed node/edge capacities plus the requested new native backing on the same timeline, including the other graph backing and all coexisting children; use the rule's formula for each growing backing. Check host byte/layout limits and petgraph's u32 sentinel exclusion before invoking `reserve_exact_nodes`/`reserve_exact_edges`. Do not let add_node/add_edge initiate amortised growth. Petgraph has no fallible reservation API: after these preflights, accept infallible exact reservation and environmental allocator failure under (`rule:nrz:cas-design-transactions`). Capacity and requested service-limit checks precede the call as ordinary errors; returned observed capacity must also be admitted before publication under (`dec:nrz:cas-backend-allocator-excess`). This retains the established graph representation without inventing allocator recovery or silently widening limits.

