**Open Question (Shared control layout authority)** · `open:nrz:cas-backend-shared-controls`

The public [Arc contract](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Arc.html) does not specify a complete allocation layout or a supported byte upper bound for its reference-count control storage and padding. Pointee layout and `size_of(Arc<T>)` therefore cannot measure that backing. This affects shared keys, frozen environments/signatures, certificates, StoreToken and namespace registrations, including surviving owners after store destruction. No supported allowance has been established for these rows; the existing STORE and registration tariffs retain their enforcement roles but do not close this gap. An owning design must obtain a supported allocation-layout guarantee or replace sharing with observable owner-managed storage while preserving identity, lifetimes and existing byte pins. Merely reading Arc internals or assuming two counters is insufficient. This remains open because changing the shared ownership representation and its callers is not the same local number conversion and may change pinned carrier layouts. It affects State.graph children (including nested exact-constant stores), State.intern, State.scalar_judgments, State.pi_judgments, State.journal, receipt_records.pending, fixed store owners and Budget.checked_records. It does not block the inventory second increment from implementing the supported projections and explicit uncovered rows; it does block lifting (`req:nrz:lc-graph-cas-backend-prerequisite`) or claiming complete physical inventory. A separate owning design must decide the shared representation, for example owner-managed shared storage with observable count slots and handles.

**Open Question (Dependency arithmetic scratch envelopes)** · `open:nrz:cas-backend-allocation-envelopes`

The retained V/S/B and graph request-envelope questions, including number-limb boxing and pending/Accounting shrink coexistence, are resolved by (`rule:nrz:cas-backend-exact-allocation`), (`dec:nrz:cas-backend-allocator-excess`) and (`dec:nrz:cas-backend-graph-allocation`). What remains is dependency-owned arithmetic scratch: the retained representation does not expose temporary BigUint/BigInt/Ratio allocation requests or their simultaneous lifetimes. Existing component/intermediate-bit and Work bounds remain, but are not a complete physical scratch projection. Any scope claiming that stronger coverage needs a supported dependency-scratch envelope or an observable arithmetic allocation route. This remaining question does not block the retained inventory's second increment or reopen the exact-retained allocation discipline; it forbids treating the final boxed result as retroactive coverage of hidden arithmetic scratch. This scratch question does not block lifting (`req:nrz:lc-graph-cas-backend-prerequisite`), which covers retained collections, but it does block any claim of complete scratch coverage. Shared controls have their separate open item above.

**Rule (Literal retained-map maintenance charges)** · `rule:nrz:cas-backend-work`

Let n be current map length, p an insertion/removal position and q complete query byte length. Use half-open binary search, one three-way midpoint comparison and immediate equality return. The search itself carries no new comparison Work under (`open:nrz:cas-global-index-search-work`).

The lookup-only charge is L, the site's former lookup-only charge, paid once before search, including a search before insertion, removal or replacement. Structural/source L is q+1; scalar/Machin L retains its existing conservative allowance; a previously uncharged private index lookup has L=0. Existing semantic caller visits are separate, not counted again as L. Other conservative existing lookup allowances remain floors. No comparison top-up or worst-case search prepayment is added. Existing floors never fall.

After search, insert an absent key for 1 Work for the new entry, remove at p for 1 Work for the returned entry, or replace a value for 2 Work. Failed lookup has no edit charge. Reuse a search position only while valid; a second search pays its former floor again. Ordered iteration retains existing caller visits without introducing searches. Right and left shifts and growth relocation carry no Work debit under (`open:nrz:cas-global-index-search-work`), because those costs depend on unrelated entries in the shared global index. The logical target is the pure function `target(n)=max(4,next_power_of_two(n))` of live length, with checked arithmetic. It is independent of observed allocator capacity and requires no inline state, preserving the pinned `Cas` layout and origin figures.

**Open Question (Global retained-index maintenance Work)** · `open:nrz:cas-global-index-search-work`

The receiving store does not charge new comparison Work for searching a global retained index, including searches before edits and inverse searches on rollback. It also does not charge shifts on insertion or removal, relocation on growth, or inverse shifts on rollback. A search or edit over an index shared with unrelated retained entries can cost more when those entries are added, making Work depend on unrelated content against this section's no-unrelated-charge rule and the walk rule that unrelated records contribute no work. The scalar/Machin site's existing floor `L=q*b(n)` already depends on map size; it remains a floor because old charges cannot fall. The local entry costs and value replacement remain charged. This open question decides how a scoped or global index can charge search and maintenance while preserving locality, including that existing size-dependent allowance.

A locality control compares the same blind-solving operation with and without unrelated retained objects under the unchanged 100,000-Work allowance. Global insertion movement can consume that allowance merely because the shared index contains more unrelated entries. With comparison and movement debits withdrawn, the same operation remains within the allowance. This is an implementation consequence of the locality rule, not a new Work allowance. The open item (`open:nrz:cas-global-index-search-work`) may later define a scoped maintenance tariff on its merits.

**Invariant (Retained-index target coverage)** · `inv:nrz:cas-backend-target-coverage`

Every installed retained-index vector backing with n live entries has actual capacity at least `target(n)`; an empty vector with no backing is the sole unallocated case. Initial allocation, checkpoint cloning, rollback transfer, and every replacement or boxed-slice copy establish this condition before publication. This makes room for an insertion without a target crossing; at a crossing, all n live entries may relocate, but relocation has no Work debit under (`open:nrz:cas-global-index-search-work`). A rollback may retain spare capacity, but its shorter live length lowers the logical target; later re-growth still checks the target without a relocation debit. The pure target follows the same 4, 8, 16, … crossing sequence as doubling on monotone insertion. On rollback it derives the smaller target from live length, while the installed backing already covers that target. A later insertion checks its next target and reserves only if physical capacity is short; checkpoint cloning may choose any adequate backing. This avoids storing a separate target field that would alter the pinned `Cas` layout. Before insertion, check `target(n+1)*size_of(T)` for representability and reserve to `target(n+1)` if necessary. A batch checks capacity at every crossing.

In the representation and inventory increments the checked byte product adds no debit to the CAS logical PayloadBytes or StagedPayloadBytes ledger and changes no byte pin. The scratch precedent (`dec:nrz:stable-collection-scratch-cost`) still governs the checked capacity arithmetic and scratch scope, without adding a global-state-dependent Work debit. Only the new complete-aggregate admission and residence checks introduced with F supply backend admission on the execution graph owner's original `ExecutionAccount` Budget: retained disjoint backend increments against its `payload_bytes`, and simultaneous retained-plus-replacement peaks against its `staged_payload_bytes`, after the overlap subtraction in (`rule:nrz:cas-backend-overlap`). This uses the existing consumer dimensions and ceilings; no new allowance or independent CAS byte budget is created. Until that integration is verified, arithmetic and capacity observations alone make no complete backend-admission claim. Allocator over-allocation does not change the local entry or replacement Work charge. All accounting arithmetic is checked. Existing semantic, key-encoding, proof, arithmetic and copy charges remain unchanged.

This integration boundary preserves existing byte pins by call-path separation, not by assuming a zero backend increment. The current ``test:unit:lc-origin-retained-staged-boundaries`` fixture in `crates/matrix/src/loan/lc_shape/tests/output/bridge.rs` invokes correspondence preparation and `LcOmegaOutput::prepare_on` directly; its retained and staged envelopes are the existing owner scopes. `LcOmegaCorrespondence::retained_bytes()` and its construct/replay/authentication/certificate methods retain their existing logical/STORE partition and reservation handoffs. The current graph-core admission in `loan/graph/walk/admission.rs` accepts only fixture nodes; it is not F's complete checked-omega admission. Consequently the new backend debit must live exclusively in F's new aggregate checks, not in those existing helpers, `ExecutionAccount::new`, or scoped graph-core admission. Existing exact/one-below byte fixtures never enter that new path and receive no new debit. When F calls an existing helper, the disjoint backend reservation is held by F on the original account, with overlap subtracted; that whole-call F boundary is new evidence, not a revision of the helper's pinned boundary.

Correspondence integration here means exposing a borrowed, allocation-identified backend inventory from its frozen CAS for F's aggregation and lifetime tracking, without altering `retained_bytes()`, B_corr, pinned carrier layout or existing helper reservations. Any projection scratch belongs to F's new scope, not an added allocation on the old pinned paths. The known-owner overlap control retains its existing assertions; its extension tests this disjoint projection, and complete-aggregate byte boundaries are new F evidence. These conditions preserve the existing byte figures of (`rule:nrz:lc-graph-retained-overlap`) and (`req:nrz:tiles-streams-graph-evidence`). If an implementing change instead requires a new debit, carrier allocation or revised byte boundary on an existing pinned path, stop and raise that path with evidence under (`open:nrz:tiles-streams-graph-admission-gates`); this design does not authorize that revision.

Budget-bearing fallible map entry points must replace private infallible searches/mutations where needed. Propagate their faults and explicit budgets through callers; no compatibility wrapper performs uncharged lookup. Public inspection requiring a retained-map search receives a caller budget in the implementation API change; direct already-authenticated slot access and bounded summaries remain distinct. Caller pin changes, even outside CAS, must be attributed solely to these map operations. Unrelated binding or matrix tariffs are outside the retained-map Work scope stated at the head of (`sec:nrz:cas-backend-inventory`).

**Rule (Rollback credit and capacity timeline)** · `rule:nrz:cas-backend-rollback`

For journaled insertions, prepay 1 Work for the inverse removal's own returned entry, without an inverse-search or inverse-shift charge (`open:nrz:cas-global-index-search-work`). Associate credit with the undo entry before publication. Reverse chronological undo removes later edits first; nested commits transfer the same prepaid entry credit to the outer log. A journaled value replacement prepays reverse replacement 2 without a search-comparison charge. Journal-free maps restored by checkpoint transfer, including `State.retained`, are exempt from inverse-search/removal/replacement prepayment: their rollback transfers the saved backing instead of running those inverses. Their forward maintenance still pays the ordinary rule. The counter checkpoint's one-per-live-entry copy remains within the existing 256 checkpoint floor ; do not charge that copy twice. Credit is already spent Work, not general remaining budget. It is never refunded, including on commit. Undo completes using it when remaining Work is zero. New undo-key copies retain their ordinary copy and allocation preflight; no hidden clone is free. Arbitrary deletion of earlier published content is not introduced; such a future operation would need an inverse insertion/growth contract.

Checkpoints, log capacity, key children, pending judgments, graph backings and replacement buffers are observed on one timeline. In F's new complete-aggregate checks, reserve requested backend growth on the original `ExecutionAccount` scope identified in (`rule:nrz:cas-backend-work`) before allocation, without inserting this debit into an existing pinned helper path. The earlier increments perform checked size arithmetic and inventory observation only, without a new CAS staged-byte debit. Record actual capacity after allocation and before publication, and distinguish requested and observed quantities. An observation after allocation cannot authorize a previously uncovered peak. The requested growth/shrink envelopes follow (`rule:nrz:cas-backend-exact-allocation`); all capacity reported after allocation, including allocator-granted excess, must be admitted before publication under (`dec:nrz:cas-backend-allocator-excess`). Unsupported shared controls and dependency arithmetic scratch retain their named gaps. Unreported allocator internals are outside this observed retained-capacity projection. Keep existing CAS logical byte dimensions and pins unchanged; the consumer reservation covers only the disjoint backend projection after its overlap is proved, not an unnamed allowance added to CAS staging.

Rollback restores logical occupancy, not automatically physical capacity. Surviving spare backend capacity keeps its identity in the owning inventory and, once integrated, its consumer reservation for the full observed capacity until actual destruction, including reported allocator-granted excess under (`dec:nrz:cas-backend-allocator-excess`). If that consumer byte scope cannot retain it, prepare reversible replacement backing before mutation and account for both old and new backings simultaneously. Any future local entry or replacement Work on that path requires its own explicit charge. Do not restore an old debit while keeping a larger allocation. The implementation reconciles existing anonymous-judgment rollback wording with this distinction between discarded payload/staging and surviving receiving-backend backing; logical authority, serial invalidation and Work non-refund remain unchanged. No byte pin is increased to hide a failed replacement or overlap check.

